# Architecture docs stay in sync with the code

How the extension and its backend work is documented in [`../../acorn/docs/architecture/extension.md`](../../acorn/docs/architecture/extension.md). A logic change is not done until that document says the same thing as the code.

## When this applies

Any change to behavior in `acorn/extension`, `acorn/packages/*`, `acorn-backend`, or the Go packages the agent shares: flows, limits and timeouts, routes, message or socket names, prompts or model questions, stored data, who sees what, or user-visible behavior. Pure refactors, renames with no behavior change, and styling do not need an update.

## What to do

1. Find the section the change touches:

| You change | Update section |
| --- | --- |
| Manifest, build, env vars, service worker, content script, side panel | §4 |
| Sign-in, sessions, support handoff, socket events | §5 |
| Per-tab storage keys or lifecycle | §6 |
| Page reading (the agent sees screenshots), what the content script does | §7 |
| The agent Run: loop, input, uploads, stopping, limits, outcomes (§8 to §10 say what was removed) | §19 |
| Résumé gate, recommend, cover letters, file formats, generate | §11 |
| acorn-backend routes, config, usage, capture, collections | §12 |
| Writer prompt, Jev questions (résumé match) | §13 |
| What data reaches which party, secrets, retention | §14 |

2. Edit that section in the same commit as the code. Change the numbers, names, and file links it quotes (limits, routes, env vars, collections).
3. If the change makes a statement in §14.4 or §15 untrue, fix or remove it. Add an entry there when you introduce a known gap.
4. Update the "Written from the code at commit ... (extension vX)" line at the top when you re-verify the whole document.
5. Keep links relative and valid, and run `bunx prettier --write` on the file.

Do not describe code you did not read. If you are unsure whether a section is still right, read the code and fix the section.
